  <body>
    <div class="cover">
      <div class="company">SALE FISH MARKETING AND CONSULTING</div>
      <div class="tagline">OT Cybersecurity Practice</div>
      <div class="title">OT Cybersecurity<br/>Assessment Report</div>
      <p style="margin-top:30pt; font-size:13pt; font-weight:bold;">Bayport Distribution Terminal</p>
      <p style="font-size:11pt;">Meridian Chemical Solutions</p>
      <p style="margin-top:20pt; font-size:12pt;">Chemical &nbsp;|&nbsp; NIST SP 800-82r3</p>
      <p style="font-size:11pt; color:#595959;">November 21, 2026</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Executive Summary</h1>
    <table class="meta-table">
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Site:</td><td>Bayport Distribution Terminal</td><td class="meta-label">Organization:</td><td>Meridian Chemical Solutions</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Industry:</td><td>Chemical</td><td class="meta-label">Standard:</td><td>NIST SP 800-82r3</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Assessment Mode:</td><td>Detailed</td><td class="meta-label">Date:</td><td>November 21, 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Assessor:</td><td>T. Jackson, Sale Fish Marketing and Consulting</td><td class="meta-label">Question Bank:</td><td>v2.1.0</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div style="margin-top:14pt;">
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">2.9</div><div class="lbl">Overall Maturity (CMM 1-5)</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">2.9</div><div class="lbl">Risk-Weighted Maturity</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">2.8</div><div class="lbl">Industry Baseline (Illustrative)</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">20</div><div class="lbl">Risk Items Identified</div></div>
    </div>

    
    <p><strong>Bayport Distribution Terminal</strong> (Meridian Chemical Solutions) — This assessment evaluated <strong>Chemical</strong> OT cybersecurity maturity against <strong>NIST SP 800-82r3</strong>, cross-referenced to NIST CSF 2.0, using the detailed question set.</p>
    <p>Overall maturity scored <strong>2.9 out of 5.0 (Managed)</strong>, at or above the illustrative Chemical sector baseline of 2.8. The risk-weighted maturity score is <strong>2.9</strong>.</p>
    <p>The weakest NIST CSF 2.0 function is <strong>Detect</strong> (2.3/5), warranting priority attention. A total of <strong>20 risk item(s)</strong> scored at or below 3.0 and are prioritized in the Top 10 Risks section below.</p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-muted); font-size:13px;">Assessed by: T. Jackson, Sale Fish Marketing and Consulting</p>
  
    <p class="note">N/A responses were excluded from scoring averages for this report. Industry baseline is an illustrative planning estimate, not empirical survey data.</p>

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Detailed Summary</h1>
    <h2>Control Family Maturity</h2>
    <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="600" style="width:600px;" />
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>ID</th><th>Control Family</th><th>NIST CSF 2.0 Cross-Reference</th><th>CTRL Ref</th><th>Score</th><th>CMM Level</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>AC</td><td>Access Control</td><td>PR.AA — Identity Management, Authentication and Access Control</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-3.3.2, CTRL-3.3.3</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3.3</td>
      <td>Defined</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>IA</td><td>Identification &amp; Authentication</td><td>PR.AA — Identity Management, Authentication and Access Control</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-2.1.1</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3.0</td>
      <td>Defined</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>SC</td><td>System &amp; Communications Protection</td><td>PR.IR — Technology Infrastructure Resilience</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-3.2.1, CTRL-3.2.2, CTRL-3.3.1</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3.5</td>
      <td>Defined</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>SI</td><td>System &amp; Information Integrity</td><td>PR.PS — Platform Security</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-2.1.3, CTRL-8.5.1</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.7</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>AU</td><td>Audit &amp; Accountability</td><td>DE.CM — Continuous Monitoring</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-6.1.1, CTRL-6.1.2, CTRL-6.4.1, CTRL-6.4.2</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.3</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>IR</td><td>Incident Response</td><td>RS.MA — Incident Management</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-7.1.1, CTRL-7.1.2, CTRL-7.2.1, CTRL-7.2.2</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.7</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>CP</td><td>Contingency Planning</td><td>RC.RP — Recovery Plan Execution</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-7.4.1, CTRL-7.4.2</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.7</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>CM</td><td>Configuration Management</td><td>PR.PS — Platform Security</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-8.2.1, CTRL-8.2.2, CTRL-8.3.1, CTRL-8.3.2, CTRL-8.3.3</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.7</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr></table>

    <h2>NIST CSF 2.0 Function Rollup</h2>
    <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="380" style="width:380px;" />
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>CSF 2.0 Function</th><th>Average Score</th></tr><tr><td>Govern (GV)</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td></tr><tr><td>Identify (ID)</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td></tr><tr><td>Protect (PR)</td><td style="text-align:center;">3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Detect (DE)</td><td style="text-align:center;">2.3</td></tr><tr><td>Respond (RS)</td><td style="text-align:center;">2.7</td></tr><tr><td>Recover (RC)</td><td style="text-align:center;">2.7</td></tr></table>

    <h2>Maturity vs. Industry Baseline</h2>
    <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="600" style="width:600px;" />

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Top 10 Risks (Prioritized)</h1>
    <p class="note">Priority score = (5 − control score) × control-family criticality weight.</p>
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>#</th><th>Control Family</th><th>Item</th><th>Score</th><th>Recommended Action</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">1</td><td>Incident Response</td><td>Regulatory incident-reporting obligations (e.g., CISA, sector-specific) are built into the IR process as an explicit step.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Document and rehearse a safety-first containment decision process (joint IR/operations/safety decision for SIS-adjacent assets). Build regulatory reporting timelines into the IR process explicitly, not as an afterthought.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">2</td><td>System &amp; Information Integrity</td><td>Firmware updates are verified against vendor cryptographic signatures before deployment.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Deploy control-logic and configuration integrity monitoring, especially on engineering workstations. Verify firmware updates against vendor cryptographic signatures before deployment. Document compensating controls for legacy devices that cannot run endpoint protection.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">3</td><td>Contingency Planning</td><td>Restore capability is actually tested on a defined cadence, not merely assumed to work.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Establish verified 'golden image' backups (logic + configuration + firmware) for high-criticality assets, refreshed on every approved change. Actually test restore capability on a defined cadence, and define RTO/RPO jointly with operations/safety.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">4</td><td>Identification &amp; Authentication</td><td>Device and software identity (certificates, keys) is managed with a defined lifecycle (issuance, rotation, revocation).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Enforce MFA on all remote and administrative OT access. Establish a certificate/key lifecycle process (issuance, rotation, revocation) for device and software identity, particularly for OPC-UA and CIP Security deployments.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">5</td><td>Configuration Management</td><td>Patches are validated in a representative staging environment before deployment to production Tier 1/2 assets.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Implement a formal Management of Change (MOC) process with risk, safety, and security review gates for every change to high-criticality assets. Maintain a current asset inventory reconciled at least annually, and validate patches in a staging environment before production deployment.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">6</td><td>Audit &amp; Accountability</td><td>OT-derived alerts are onboarded into a SIEM/SOC with OT-aware correlation logic, not generic IT rules.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Deploy passive (SPAN/TAP) network monitoring at every zone conduit. Onboard OT alerts into the SIEM/SOC with OT-aware correlation logic and ensure analysts have asset-criticality context before triaging.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">7</td><td>Audit &amp; Accountability</td><td>Protocol-aware detection (Modbus, DNP3, OPC-UA, EtherNet/IP, PROFINET function codes) is in place, not just IP/port-level visibility.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Deploy passive (SPAN/TAP) network monitoring at every zone conduit. Onboard OT alerts into the SIEM/SOC with OT-aware correlation logic and ensure analysts have asset-criticality context before triaging.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">8</td><td>System &amp; Communications Protection</td><td>Inter-zone conduits use default-deny, explicit allow-list firewall rules rather than broad 'any-any' access.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3/5</td><td>Establish a Level 3.5 industrial DMZ with brokered patch relay, jump host, and historian mirror functions — no direct session traversal between OT and IT. Implement default-deny conduit allow-lists between zones, and segment SIS as its own zone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">9</td><td>Incident Response</td><td>Containment decisions for OT incidents follow a documented, safety-reviewed process rather than automatic/unilateral isolation.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3/5</td><td>Document and rehearse a safety-first containment decision process (joint IR/operations/safety decision for SIS-adjacent assets). Build regulatory reporting timelines into the IR process explicitly, not as an afterthought.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">10</td><td>Incident Response</td><td>Pre-approved emergency isolation procedures exist and have been rehearsed for your highest-criticality zones.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3/5</td><td>Document and rehearse a safety-first containment decision process (joint IR/operations/safety decision for SIS-adjacent assets). Build regulatory reporting timelines into the IR process explicitly, not as an afterthought.</td>
    </tr></table>

